``` Introduction

`Table` (`ore/table.ore`) is an ActiveRecord-style ORM on top of `ore/database.ore` -- see learn/database.ore first if `Sqlite`/`@connect` are new to you. Compose a type with `Table`, point its static `../database` at a connected database, and give it a `table_name`.
```
@load 'ore/database.ore'
@load 'ore/table.ore'

db := Sqlite(':memory:')
@connect db

db.create_table('survivors', {
	id: 'primary_key',
	name: 'String'
})

Survivor | Table {
	../database := ~/db
	table_name := 'survivors'
}


``` infer_table_name_from_class!

Instead of writing `table_name` out by hand, an instance can derive it from its own composed type name -- `Survivor` becomes `'survivors'`.
```
s := Survivor()
s.infer_table_name_from_class!()
@assert s.table_name == 'survivors', 'infer_table_name_from_class! should derive the table name from the composed type name'


``` create and all

`create` inserts a row and returns its new id. `all` fetches every row back as an Array of Dictionaries.
```
jack_id := Survivor.create({name: 'Jack'})
Survivor.create({name: 'Kate'})

@assert Survivor.all() == [{id: 1, name: 'Jack'}, {id: 2, name: 'Kate'}], 'all should fetch every row as an Array of Dictionaries'


``` find, find_by, where

`find` looks up by id. `find_by` returns the first row matching a Dictionary of conditions, or nil. `where` returns every matching row.
```
@assert Survivor.find(jack_id) == {id: 1, name: 'Jack'}, 'find should look up a row by id'
@assert Survivor.find_by({name: 'Kate'}) == {id: 2, name: 'Kate'}, 'find_by should return the first row matching the conditions'
@assert Survivor.where({name: 'Jack'}) == [{id: 1, name: 'Jack'}], 'where should return every matching row'


``` update and delete

```
Survivor.update(jack_id, {name: 'Jack Shephard'})
@assert Survivor.find(jack_id) == {id: 1, name: 'Jack Shephard'}, 'update should change the row in place'

Survivor.delete(jack_id)
@assert Survivor.all() == [{id: 2, name: 'Kate'}], 'delete should remove the row'
